      "id": 1,
      "text": "Harris Township was named after Menasippe Perron, who built a dam and sawmill there in 1883 and became its first postmaster.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harris_Township%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A railroad cut through Harris in 1873 to haul ore and timber, marking the start of its logging boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harris_Township%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Cedar River and its tributaries drain the township’s southern third, while the Bark River begins along its eastern edge.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harris_Township%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Ford River empties the northern part of Harris, with Tenmile Creek feeding its central basin.",
